Here the heap is built one item at a time instead of heapifying all parent nodes at once. Each new value moves upward while it is larger than its parent. Once the max heap is ready, the normal root swap and push-down steps finish the ordering.

This is a little slower to build than bottom-up heap construction, but the steps are easy to follow. The array is modified in place, including when it has duplicate or negative values.

```csharp
public class Solution {
    public void heapSort(int[] nums) {
        int heapSize = 0;

        for (int index = 0; index < nums.Length; index++) {
            heapSize++;
            MoveUp(nums, index);
        }

        for (int end = nums.Length - 1; end > 0; end--) {
            Swap(nums, 0, end);
            heapSize--;
            MoveDown(nums, 0, heapSize);
        }
    }

    private void MoveUp(int[] nums, int index) {
        while (index > 0) {
            int parent = (index - 1) / 2;
            if (nums[parent] >= nums[index]) {
                break;
            }

            Swap(nums, parent, index);
            index = parent;
        }
    }

    private void MoveDown(int[] nums, int index, int heapSize) {
        while (true) {
            int left = index * 2 + 1;
            int right = left + 1;
            int largest = index;

            if (left < heapSize && nums[left] > nums[largest]) {
                largest = left;
            }
            if (right < heapSize && nums[right] > nums[largest]) {
                largest = right;
            }

            if (largest == index) {
                return;
            }

            Swap(nums, index, largest);
            index = largest;
        }
    }

    private void Swap(int[] nums, int first, int second) {
        int temp = nums[first];
        nums[first] = nums[second];
        nums[second] = temp;
    }
}
```

Each insertion can take `O(log n)`, and each removal also takes `O(log n)`, giving `O(n log n)` time overall. Only a few variables are used, so the extra space is `O(1)`.

This version keeps the heap repair step recursive. The largest child is selected, and the current value moves down until the max-heap rule is true again.

A small dry run: for `[2, 5, 1]`, heapifying places `5` at the root. Swapping it with the last position gives `[1, 2, 5]`, then the shorter prefix is repaired to finish as `[1, 2, 5]`.

The input list is sorted in place. Empty input and repeated values are also handled by the same loop conditions.

