The running time is `O(n log n)`. The recursive calls use `O(log n)` stack space in the worst case, so this version is not strictly constant extra space.

I used a max heap so the largest value reaches the end of the array after each removal. First, the array is turned into a max heap. Then the root is swapped with the last unsorted value, and the heap is repaired on the smaller range.

The array is changed in place. An empty array and a one-element array need no special work because the loops simply do not run.

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    void heapSort(vector<int>& nums) {
        int n = static_cast<int>(nums.size());

        for (int i = n / 2 - 1; i >= 0; --i) {
            siftDown(nums, i, n);
        }

        for (int end = n - 1; end > 0; --end) {
            swap(nums[0], nums[end]);
            siftDown(nums, 0, end);
        }
    }

private:
    void siftDown(vector<int>& nums, int root, int size) {
        while (true) {
            int largest = root;
            int left = 2 * root + 1;
            int right = 2 * root + 2;

            if (left < size && nums[left] > nums[largest]) {
                largest = left;
            }
            if (right < size && nums[right] > nums[largest]) {
                largest = right;
            }

            if (largest == root) {
                break;
            }

            swap(nums[root], nums[largest]);
            root = largest;
        }
    }
};
```

Time complexity: `O(n log n)`.

Space complexity: `O(1)` apart from the input array.

Instead of keeping the largest value at the root, this one builds a min heap. The smallest value is removed from the root and written from the end toward the beginning. Writing backward is what gives the final array its increasing order.

For `[3, 1, 2]`, the min heap first gives `1`, which goes to index `0` after the remaining length is considered through the backward placement. Each root removal shortens the heap, and the next smallest value is selected.

This uses the input list as the heap storage, so there is no separate result list. The heap is changed while sorting.

