The final reverse is needed because the min-heap extraction places values in decreasing order from left to right. Time complexity is `O(n log n)`, and extra space is `O(1)`.

The heap can be built from the last parent toward the root. Leaves already behave like small heaps, so only the parent nodes need to be pushed down. After that, the root is the next largest number.

For example, with `[4, 1, 3, 2]`, the root is moved to the end when it is the largest value. The remaining prefix is then fixed as another heap. This code modifies `nums` directly.

```java
class Solution {
    public void heapSort(int[] nums) {
        int n = nums.length;

        for (int parent = n / 2 - 1; parent >= 0; parent--) {
            pushDown(nums, parent, n);
        }

        for (int last = n - 1; last > 0; last--) {
            int temp = nums[0];
            nums[0] = nums[last];
            nums[last] = temp;
            pushDown(nums, 0, last);
        }
    }

    private void pushDown(int[] nums, int index, int heapSize) {
        while (index < heapSize / 2) {
            int left = index * 2 + 1;
            int right = left + 1;
            int biggerChild = left;

            if (right < heapSize && nums[right] > nums[left]) {
                biggerChild = right;
            }

            if (nums[index] >= nums[biggerChild]) {
                break;
            }

            int temp = nums[index];
            nums[index] = nums[biggerChild];
            nums[biggerChild] = temp;
            index = biggerChild;
        }
    }
}
```

The heap construction is linear, while removing all elements costs `O(n log n)`, so the total time is `O(n log n)`. The extra space is `O(1)`.

## Intuition

To sort the array in ascending order, we first arrange it as a max-heap. In a max-heap, the largest element is always at the root.

We can then swap the root with the last element of the current heap. This places the largest remaining element in its final sorted position. After reducing the heap’s size, we restore the max-heap property and repeat until the array is sorted.

## Approach

1. Build a max-heap by applying `heapifyDown` to every non-leaf node, starting from the last non-leaf node and moving toward the root.
2. Set `last` to the final index of the array. It represents the end of the current heap.
3. While the heap contains more than one element:
   - Swap the root with the element at `last`.
   - Decrease `last` so the sorted element is excluded from the heap.
   - Apply `heapifyDown` to the root to restore the max-heap property.
4. In `heapifyDown`, compare the current node with its children and swap it with the larger child when necessary.

